(vector (map (:db/id -1)
 (:adr/id "adr-2609077000-amus-definition-cids-are-unison-shaped-measured")
 (:adr/doc_type "adr")
 (:adr/topic "amus-definition-cids-are-unison-shaped-measured")
 (:adr/title "Amu's definition CIDs are Unison-shaped — measured, with the two claims that were not")
 (:adr/status "accepted")
 (:adr/date "2026-09-07")
 (:adr/deciders (vector "Jun Kawasaki" "Claude Opus 5"))
 (:adr/repos (vector "com-junkawasaki/root" "kotoba-lang/amu" "kotoba-lang/kotoba-kir" "kotoba-lang/kotoba-lang"))
 (:adr/related (vector "adr-2609076000-kir-is-an-ipld-codec-schema-and-adl" "2607289500"))
 (:adr/validation (map (:route "bin/amu, JVM-free (nbb); no clojure and no JDK invoked") (:base "kotoba-lang/amu origin/main 78cdc2e1") (:properties-checked 7) (:properties-held 7) (:doc-defects-found 2) (:compiler-defects-found 0) (:landed "kotoba-lang/amu PR #859, ADR-0342")))
 (:adr/body "## Context\n\nThe owner asked whether Amu actually hashes definitions correctly, and whether it is genuinely Unison-shaped. The answer had been read off the README in this workspace before; this time it was measured. Seven properties, on `kotoba-lang/amu` `origin/main` at `78cdc2e1`, all through `bin/amu --jvm-free` so no `clojure` and no JDK was invoked.\n\n## What was measured\n\nProbe modules are three definitions deep -- `scale` <- `bump` <- `total`, with `total` exported -- so a transitive property has somewhere to show.\n\n| # | Property | Result |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | every top-level definition gets a CID, and its `:dependencies` are CIDs rather than names | held |\n| 2 | **renaming** the private function, its parameter and its call site leaves all three CIDs **byte-identical** | held |\n| 3 | changing one body moves that definition, its caller, **and its caller's caller** whose source text is untouched | held |\n| 4 | **widening a record** used as a parameter type moves the reader's CID, with its body expression unchanged | held |\n| 5 | the CID closure keys Wasm emission: the rename pair compiles to **byte-identical** wasm, the body-change pair does not | held |\n| 6 | the emission cache answers from the CID key | held **on the worker route** -- see defect 2 |\n| 7 | a definition the identity cannot seal gets a marker, never an invented CID | held, but **not for the documented reason** -- see defect 1 |\n\nProperties 2, 3 and 4 together are the Unison model, and 5 is the part Unison does not have to prove because it has no separate emitter: the hash is not decoration, it is what the compiler keys emission on. Property 3 is the control for property 2 -- without it, byte-identical CIDs under renaming would be consistent with a hash that reads nothing.\n\n## Decision\n\n**The premise is confirmed. Amu's definition identity is Unison-shaped and it works.** Names are not in the hash on either side of a call: binders are renamed to de Bruijn positions and a callee symbol is replaced by its CID. Mutual recursion is hashed as a unit under `scc-v1`. Beyond Unison, the seal also carries the **effect row** and the **interface** including the transitive schema closure, which is why property 4 holds -- widening a record changes what every function taking one means.\n\nDocumentation elsewhere may state this as background. **It may not state it as measured unless it names the route and the base commit**, because the two things that were wrong here were both documentation, and both were confidently written.\n\n### Defect 1 -- a retired rule outlived its own amendment\n\nAmu's README said a definition the identity cannot seal is *today, one whose effect row carries the tracked control effect `:abort`, which names no capability*. Measured, an aborting definition gets a real CID and reports `:effect-row #{:abort}`.\n\nThe compiler is right and the README was four days stale. kotoba-kir `984a507` (2026-09-02) put `:abort` in the closed `control-effects` set; kotoba-lang adjudicated it; **amu ADR-0300 section 4 was amended on 2026-09-03**, naming the pin it advanced and the arguments that survive. Only the README kept the old rule -- and the README is the document a new reader opens first. `lang/code-identity.edn`, the protocol authority, was already correct: it states the refusal domain as *a wire id the catalog cannot name*.\n\nThis is the shape root ADR-2609062600 named for `AGENTS.md`: the supersede was recorded correctly and **the old rule stayed somewhere else, silently**. It is worth recording that the pattern recurred nine days later in a different repository, because the lesson is not about `AGENTS.md`.\n\n### Defect 2 -- a documented number that fails on the documented command\n\nAmu's README said renaming a private function means *the compile is served from cache and reports `:definitions-recompiled 0`*. Measured through `bin/amu compile --jvm-free`, an unchanged module compiled twice reports `3` both times.\n\nNothing is broken. The one-shot CLI has no cache **by design** -- `compile-uncached!` carries a comment saying that *the cache was not consulted* and *the cache missed* are different facts and must not print the same -- and the cache lives in `amu worker`. Driven through the worker's NDJSON protocol the claim holds exactly, and better than documented: an unchanged recompile answers `:cache :hit` with `0`, and the **renamed** module answers `:cache :miss` with `0`. That pair is the interesting one. The artifact key moved because the source text moved, so the whole-artifact cache missed; the emission stage still had nothing to re-emit, because the definition CIDs had not moved. A body change in the same worker answers `:cache :miss` with every definition re-emitted.\n\nThe defect is that the README stated a number without its invocation. A reader who measures the documented value on the documented command gets a different answer, and the first conclusion available to them is that the cache does not work. **When standing prose states a value a reader can measure, it names the command that produces it** -- the same rule this workspace already applies to not writing measured constants into prose, in its other direction.\n\n## What is NOT claimed\n\n- **JVM/nbb CID parity was not re-measured.** Amu's README claims the JVM route and the JDK-free nbb route mint byte-identical CIDs, and the repository has tests for it. Everything above ran on the nbb route only. Citing a test is not measuring it.\n- No compiler defect was found. Both findings are documentation.\n- The record-widening probe used the explicit `[:record :ns/Name [[:field :type] ...]]` parameter descriptor, which spells the schema at the use site. A shorter form may exist; four guesses were refused and the form was taken from a working fixture rather than invented.\n- Nothing here is a gate. Nothing checks that a README claim still matches the compiler, and this ADR does not invent that check.\n\n## Reproduce\n\n    A=orgs/kotoba-lang/amu/bin/amu    # read origin/main; the shared checkout runs behind\n    node $A definition-cids <file> --jvm-free\n    node $A compile <file> --target wasm32 --jvm-free --output <out>\n    node $A worker --target wasm32 < requests.ndjson   # NDJSON: {id, args:[...]}\n\nFor property 2, rename the private function, its parameter **and** its call site in one edit, then compare the CID lines and `shasum` the two wasm outputs. For property 3, change one arithmetic literal and confirm the two-hop caller moved. For property 4, widen the record in both the `defrecord` and the parameter descriptor and confirm the reader moved with an unchanged body. Do not skip property 3 or 5's negative half: a rename test that only ever reports agreement cannot be told from a test that never ran.\n\n## Landed\n\nkotoba-lang/amu PR #859 (README both paragraphs, ADR-0342). The amu ADR carries the repository-local decisions; this one carries the verification and the two rules the verification produced.")))
